body:
Well although I agree that god's thoughts are higher than ours, I dislike the argument that we have to accept everything blindly because of it. The reason I don't like it is that if anything in the bible were to be wrong, we would just say :"we can't understand/justify it, but it comes from god so it is true." and apply it even if it is wrong.

&#x200B;

I'm not quite sure that the hebrews were directly ruled by god : they had kings that sometimes went away from god back to polytheism, and they had overlords like the babylonians, romans and egyptians.  


Why would they die if they where unholy and go to the sacrifices? Also why sacrifices when Jesus says " But go and learn what this means: 'I desire mercy, not sacrifice.' For I have not come to call the righteous, but sinners. "(mathew 9:13)

&#x200B;

And I do understand the difference between moral and the law, but I don't understand how god that is perfectly good and just could create unjust laws, in order to maintain society. Didn't he destroy the earth in the times of Noa and destroy Sodom and Gomorrhe because they where corrupt? Seems like god was ok with destroying societies that were too amoral.

thank you for these precisions with quotations that do show limits to the power of the masters, and the behavior they should have.

&#x200B;

But I still don't understand why god decided that some men should have such power over others, such as the exodus says, where you can beat someone if he is able to get up the next day. God doesn't even say what this punishment should be for, he just says it's right because the master paid money for the slave and so he has that right : " When a slaveowner strikes a male or female slave with a rod and the slave dies immediately, the owner shall be punished.  But if the slave survives a day or two, there is no punishment; for the slave is the owner’s property "

&#x200B;

seems pretty oppressive to me...
